# Copy to .env and adjust. .env is git-ignored.
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=4000
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# Postgres — port 5433, not the usual 5432.
#
# Many Linux installs (Ubuntu/Debian) start a system postgres on 5432 at boot,
# so docker-compose publishes ours on 5433 to avoid the clash. If you point this
# at 5432 you will silently connect to your machine's own postgres, which has no
# renki database, and every query will fail with "relation does not exist".
#
# Running the API *inside* compose? The host becomes `db` and the port 5432 —
# compose sets that for you, so you never need to change this file.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://renki:renki@localhost:5433/renki

# Google auth
#
# Leave a value EMPTY and the app throws at startup — env.ts treats "" as
# missing, and neither of the first two has a fallback. That is deliberate:
# booting with no client ID would accept tokens minted for any Google app.

# OAuth 2.0 Web application client from console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials.
# Public — it ships to every browser. http://localhost:3000 must be an
# authorised JavaScript origin. No redirect URI is needed; this flow verifies an
# ID token rather than redirecting.
CLIENT_ID=

# Not used by the ID-token flow. If something starts needing it, the code has
# drifted to the authorisation-code flow and should be reconsidered.
CLIENT_SECRET=

# openssl rand -base64 32
# Signs Renki's own session JWTs. Never commit a real one; rotating it logs
# every user out.
JWT_SECRET=

JWT_EXPIRES_IN=7d

# Checked against the `hd` claim, not the email suffix — see auth.service.ts.
ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAIN=northsouth.edu

# ---- Private object storage (Supabase S3-compatible API) ----
#
# Holds ONE thing: a gender-challenge photo, from the moment a student uploads
# it until a moderator rules. Deleted immediately after, whichever way the
# decision went. Nothing else in Renki stores an image.
#
# Leave these blank locally. Empty selects an in-memory store, which is what
# lets `npm run dev` and CI run the whole challenge flow with no cloud account
# — the admin screen renders a data: URI exactly like the real thing.
#
# In production they are REQUIRED: storage.service.ts throws at startup rather
# than let the in-memory store ship, because that would drop every photo on
# restart and silently empty the moderator queue.
#
# NEVER prefix these NEXT_PUBLIC_. That inlines a value into the browser bundle
# at build time, and these keys bypass Supabase RLS entirely.
#
# Supabase dashboard -> Storage -> the bucket must be PRIVATE (Public: OFF).
# Project Settings -> Storage -> S3 access keys for the pair below.
STORAGE_ENDPOINT=https://<project-ref>.storage.supabase.co/storage/v1/s3
# Must match the project's region EXACTLY. SigV4 signs the region into the
# request, so a wrong one yields signed URLs that 403 with no useful error.
STORAGE_REGION=
STORAGE_BUCKET=
STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# How long a moderator's link to a challenge photo lives. Short because the
# admin page refetches rather than caching, and a link that outlives the page
# is a credential with an expiry nobody is watching.
SIGNED_URL_TTL_SECONDS=300
